case_vignette:
A 46-year-old male was found unconscious in the field and brought to the emergency department by EMS. The patient was intubated in transit and given a 2 liter bolus of normal saline. On arrival, the patient's blood pressure is 80/60 mmHg and temperature is 37.5C. Jugular veins are flat and capillary refill time is 4 seconds.

Vascular parameters are measured and are as follows:
Cardiac index - Low;
Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) - Low;
Systemic vascular resistance - High.

A 59-year-old woman presented with 2 days of left orbital pain. Medical history was significant for hypertension controlled with lisinopril and several months of epiphora without prior dacryocystitis.

Physical Examination: Examination revealed:- Visual acuity: 20/25 OD and 20/400 OS- Edematous, hyperemic left eyelids- Diffuse limitation in extraocular movements in the left eye- Normal intraocular pressure- Normal fundi on both sides

Diagnostic Tests: {'Imaging Studies': {'Initial CT with contrast': {'Title': 'Coronal Computed Tomography Scan', 'Description': 'Left-sided lacrimal sac abscess with postseptal fat stranding largely limited to the extraconal space, minimal sinus mucosal thickening, and opacification of both nasolacrimal ducts'}, 'Follow-up CT': {'Title': 'Repeat Contrast-Enhanced Computed Tomography', 'Description': 'Increased abscess size with globe compression. Minimal intraconal fat stranding, and unremarkable optic nerve appearance'}, 'MRI/MRA': {'Title': 'Magnetic Resonance Imaging and Angiography', 'Description': 'Patent ophthalmic artery; diffusion-weighted imaging showed acute injury to the entire orbital segment of the left optic nerve'}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Cultures': 'Initial cultures isolated gram-positive cocci in chains; later identified as pan-susceptible Streptococcus pyogenes'}}

A man in his 70s with no history of chronic or allergic rhinosinusitis presented to the emergency department with a headache for 2 weeks and vision loss in the right eye for 2 days. Three weeks prior, he had rhinorrhea and was diagnosed with COVID-19. He recovered from COVID-19 but developed a worsening right-sided headache 1 week later and right vision loss 2 days prior to presentation.

Physical Examination: Right eye visual acuity was limited to hand-motion detection, with severe pain on ocular motion.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Imaging Studies': {'Maxillofacial CT': {'Findings': 'Opacification of all sinuses on the right with diffuse inflammation. Bony erosion of the medial orbital apex bone, with extension of inflammation into the apex', 'Images': {'Title': 'Contrast-Enhanced Computed Tomographic Scans', 'Description': 'Complete opacification of the (A) right frontal sinus, (B) right ethmoid sinus with erosion of the medial orbital apex bone, extension of inflammation into the apex, and an air-fluid level in the sphenoid sinus, and (C) right maxillary sinus'}}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Serum IgG4': '277 mg/dL (normal, <123 mg/dL)', 'Sinus Cultures': 'Positive for Streptococcus constellatus'}, 'Pathology': {'Findings': 'Dense infiltrate of IgG-containing plasma cells, with most being IgG4 positive', 'Images': {'Title': 'Histopathologic Images', 'Description': 'A, Dense band-like infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells present beneath the epithelium. B, IgG stain demonstrates 50 to 100 plasma cells in many high-power fields. C, Most of the IgG-containing plasma cells were IgG4 positive'}}}

A 62-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis treated with fingolimod for 3 years presented with a tender nodule on her forehead, which had gradually grown over 3 weeks. She reported bumping her forehead on an air-conditioning unit several months prior. She denied fever, neck stiffness, and photophobia. She lived alone with a pet cat and spent minimal time outdoors. She had no recent exposure to systemic steroids.

Physical Examination: - Neurological examination was at baseline- Eroded, crusted papule on the left forehead- No other lesions on full-body skin examination

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': '- White blood cell count: 3.9/μL (normal 4000-1100/μL)- Lymphocyte count: 0.65/μL (normal 1000-5000/μL)- Absolute CD4 count: 56/μL (normal 560-1840/μL)- CD8 count: 121/μL (normal 260-1230/μL)- HIV test: Negative- Serum cryptococcal antigen: Negative- Blood cultures: Negative- Cerebrospinal fluid cryptococcal antigen: Negative- Cerebrospinal fluid cultures: Negative', 'Imaging Studies': '- Chest radiography: Negative', 'Pathology': '- Skin biopsy: Granulomatous inflammation composed of histiocytes, giant cells, and lymphocytes admixed with numerous narrow-budding yeasts with thick capsules- Tissue culture: Grew Cryptococcus neoformansImage Title: Cutaneous Cryptococcus Clinical and Histologic PhotographsImage Description: A) Eroded, crusted papule on the left forehead. B) Skin biopsy specimen showing granulomatous lymphohistiocytic inflammation surrounding thickly encapsulated yeasts'}

A teenage girl presented with asymptomatic, punctate, keratotic papules localized to the right palm and right third proximal finger, present since age 2 years. Prior treatments included cryotherapy, pulsed dye laser, and 40% salicylic acid, with partial improvement reported but never complete clearance. Medical history and family history were unremarkable.

Physical Examination: Grouped and circumscribed, firm, skin-colored, 1- to 3-mm papules with a central punctate dark core on the right palm and finger.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Histopathology': {'Procedure': '4-mm punch biopsy from a papule on the palm', 'Findings': 'Dilated cystic spaces with mild squamous hyperplasia and central laminated keratin resembling a dilated terminal hair follicle infundibulum', 'Images': {'Figure A and B': 'Grouped, dome-shaped, skin-colored, punctate papules with central keratotic plugging localized to the right palm and finger', 'Figure C and D': 'Dilated follicular infundibulum with keratinous core and absent hair shaft (hematoxylin-eosin)'}}}

A 51-year-old woman presented with asymmetrical eyelid tumefactions. Six years earlier, she had episodes of pruriginous inflammatory edema of the eyelid. By the time of admission, the eyelid edema had become permanent and progressively became rock hard to palpation, which severely limited vision.

Physical Examination: Rock-hard eyelid tumefactions on palpation with severely limited vision

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': ['- Elevated total IgE level (1819.2 µg/L)', '- Polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia (1.74 g/dL)', '- Elevated IgG1, IgG2, and IgG4 levels (IgG4: 202.53 mg/dL)', '- Negative results for: antinuclear antibodies (ANAs), anti–double-stranded DNA antibodies (anti-dsDNAs), antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCAs), anti-Ro/SSAs, anti-La/SSBs, and complement activity'], 'Imaging Studies': ['- MRI: Revealed periocular infiltration of the superior eyelids, including the lacrimal glands', '- Chest, abdomen, and pelvis CT and PET: Ruled out extraocular manifestations'], 'Pathological Findings': ['Initial eyelid biopsy: Perivascular and interstitial infiltrate composed of polynuclear eosinophils and neutrophils', 'Deep eyelid biopsy revealed:', '- Storiform-type fibrosis', '- Dense inflammatory infiltrate composed of plasma and eosinophil cells around vessels without obliterative phlebitis', '- Most plasma cells expressed IgG, with an IgG4/IgG ratio greater than 60%', '- 116 IgG4-positive plasma cells counted per high-power field', 'Accessory salivary gland biopsy: Excluded Sjögren syndrome or sarcoidosis'], 'Images': [{'Title': 'Clinical Images of Bilateral Pseudotumoral Eyelid Tumefactions', 'Description': 'Shows bilateral eyelid tumefactions before and after treatment'}, {'Title': 'Biopsy Specimens From the Eyelid', 'Description': 'A) Storiform fibrosis with lymphoblastic and eosinophilic infiltrate (×40)\nB) IgG4 positivity with IgG4+/IgG+ plasma cell ratio greater than 60% (×10)'}]}

A woman in her 30s presented with a chronic history of slowly expanding asymptomatic papules on the dorsal hands since childhood, with more recent involvement of both forearms. She denied photosensitivity, arthritis, or muscle weakness.

Physical Examination: Multiple discrete reddish-brown, well-defined, smooth 2- to 6-mm dome-shaped papules. There were no nail changes or mucous membrane involvement.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': 'Complete blood cell count, lipid profile, serum protein electrophoresis, antinuclear antibody, extractable nuclear antigen, and serum tryptase were all unremarkable.', 'Histopathology': 'Revealed a CD163- and CD68PG-positive diffuse dermal histiocytic infiltrate with scattered mast cells and abundant mucin. Results of S-100 protein and CD1a stains were negative.', 'Electron Microscopy': 'Showed concentric electron dense lamellar inclusion bodies in the cytoplasm of histiocytes.', 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Dome-shaped papules on the hand and forearm', 'Figure B and C': 'Shave biopsy specimens show dermal interstitial infiltrate and dermal mucinosis with mast cells (hematoxylin and eosin)', 'Figure D': 'Scanning electronic microscopic findings show intracytoplasmic zebra bodies at 25 kilovolts'}}

A man in his 30s with no medical history of illness presented with a lesion on his left scalp. The patient noted that the lesion had slowly enlarged over the preceding 5 years from when he first noticed it as a small bump. He denied a history of trauma prior to its development. The patient denied any spontaneous bleeding, crusting, or itching, and he was primarily concerned about its growth. There was no personal or family history of malignancy.

Physical Examination: The patient's left frontal scalp revealed a mobile 1.2-cm mammillated, soft, brown plaque with protruding terminal hairs. The remainder of the patient's skin examination revealed no other concerning lesions. There was no lymphadenopathy present.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Dermoscopic Examination': 'The lesion was characterized by irregular pigment globules, milialike cysts, and focal arborizing vessels.', 'Histopathologic Examination': 'Revealed epidermal projections composed of monotonous basaloid and squamous epithelial cells. Some tumor islands contained mature sebocytes and ductal structures with an eosinophilic cuticle. Areas of pigmented poroid cells and cystic structures correlated with dermoscopic brown globules and milialike cysts.', 'Images': [{'Title': 'Figure A', 'Description': 'A brown mammilated plaque on the left frontal scalp'}, {'Title': 'Figure B', 'Description': 'Polarized dermoscopic image of the plaque'}, {'Title': 'Figure C', 'Description': 'Shave biopsy specimen of the lesion (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×40)'}, {'Title': 'Figure D', 'Description': 'Shave biopsy specimen of the lesion at a greater magnification (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×400)'}]}

A man in his 80s presented with an 18-month history of skin changes. The patient complained about a mild tingling sensation within the lesion. He denied any triggers, causes, aggravating or alleviating factors, and had not attempted any treatments. Review of systems was negative except for easy bruising. Current medications included finasteride, montelukast, enalapril, and metoprolol. Medical history was significant for right total reverse shoulder replacement 5 years prior.

Physical Examination: An irregularly linear, rusty-brown, atrophic, streaklike plaque extending from the tip of his right shoulder to the right biceps, where it ended in arborizing purple plaque with scattered, slightly indurated, irregular papules within it. There was no warmth, thrills, or pulsations across the affected area. Apart from the described lesion, physical examination was unremarkable, and he had full range of motion of his right shoulder.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Histopathology': {'Punch Biopsy Findings': 'Dense, nodular infiltrate of lymphocytes and histiocytes throughout the dermis and within thin-walled lymphatic vessels.', 'Special Stains': ['Immunohistochemical staining for CD68 antigen revealed infiltrate composed mostly of histiocytes', 'Immunohistochemical stain for D2-40 antigen highlighted thin-walled lymphatic vessels'], 'Images': {'Figure A and B': 'Clinical photographs showing the linear, rusty-brown, atrophic, streaklike plaque with arborizing purple plaque', 'Figure C': 'Hematoxylin-eosin staining showing dermal infiltrate arranged in nodules with clefting between nodules; inflammatory aggregates within dilated vessels', 'Figure D': 'Immunohistochemical staining for CD68 antigen showing histiocytic infiltrate'}}}

A man in his 60s presented with a 2-year history of gradual-onset right facial weakness leading to paralysis. He was initially treated with oral steroids. At presentation, he denied hearing loss, tinnitus, imbalance, or other cranial nerve deficits. He had no other significant medical history.

Physical Examination: Patient had complete right facial paralysis and a nonpulsatile mass protruding through the inferior ear canal. Audiographic findings showed mild sensorineural loss at 4000 Hz, but were otherwise normal.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Imaging Studies': {'MRI': '- MRI scan showed a 5 × 10-mm irregular lesion in the right mastoid near the stylomastoid foramen that had heterogeneous enhancement on T1 with contrast.\n- Follow-up MRI (9 months post-op) showed heterogeneous signal in the stylomastoid foramen on T2 images', 'CT': 'Imaging showed expansion of the fallopian canal distal to the second genu of the facial nerve, erosion of the posterior external auditory canal, and expansion of the stylomastoid foramen'}, 'Pathology': {'Gross Examination': '2 × 1.5 × 0.5-cm tan to brown, rubbery specimen consisted of cartilage', 'Microscopic Examination': 'Pleomorphic hyperchromatic nuclei, staining positive for S100 and negative for KI-67. Low cellularity and low MIB-1 proliferation index', 'Image Description': 'Microscopic image showing increased numbers of atypical pleomorphic nuclei in a myxochondroid matrix. Hyperchromasia and binucleate forms were found although mitoses were not'}}

A male adolescent with skin manifestations since infancy and multiple hospitalizations. Throughout his life, his condition initially responded to multiple systemic and biologic agents that subsequently failed. Current medications included oral methotrexate, 10 mg/wk, and intravenous infusion of infliximab, 5 mg/kg, every 6 weeks for the previous 9 months.

Physical Examination: Examination revealed an ill-appearing patient with erythroderma and pustules covering 70% of his body.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': ['- Markedly elevated C-reactive protein', '- Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate', '- Elevated white blood cell count', '- Elevated body temperature', '- Skin culture: positive for Group B Streptococcus', '- Blood and urine cultures: negative for organisms', '- High titer neutralizing antibodies against infliximab detected'], 'Genetic Testing': ['- Genomic DNA analysis revealed homozygous mutation within the IL36RN gene at position c.115+6T>C', "- Heterozygous c.115+6T>C mutation confirmed in patient's mother"], 'Immunological Studies': ['- Flow cytometric analysis of lesional skin showed markedly high production of IL-17A from CD4+ T cells', '- Approximately 25-fold more IL-17A–producing CD4+ T cells compared to healthy donor', '- Minimal differences in tumor necrosis factor, interferon γ, and IL-13']}

A 77-year-old man initially presented in 2011 with tense bullae on the back and extremities. After treatment and being symptom-free for 5 years, in 2017 he developed new erosions and tense bullae, mainly on the palate and feet. During hospitalization, the patient developed a progressive tetraparesis, mild paresthesia, and respiratory failure complicated by pneumonia.

Physical Examination: Clinical findings in 2017 showed:- Tense, hemorrhagic bullae on the foot- Bullae and erosions on the oral mucosa- Progressive tetraparesis- Mild paresthesia- Respiratory symptoms

Diagnostic Tests: {'Histopathology': 'Neutrophil- and eosinophil-rich infiltrate and subepidermal bulla (magnification ×20)', 'Immunological Studies': {'Direct Immunofluorescence': 'Linear deposition of IgG at the epidermal basal membrane and weak suprabasal weblike deposition of IgG (perilesional, oil immersion, magnification ×63)', 'Indirect Immunofluorescence': 'IgG binding to both roof and floor of artificial bulla in human salt-split skin', 'Antibody Tests': '- IgG reactivity against p200 and laminin-332 protein- Anti-CNTN1 autoantibodies positive- Anti-LGi1 autoantibodies positive- Anti-BP180-NC16A antibodies negative'}, 'Images': {'Title': 'Clinical, Histological, and Immunohistological Findings in 2017', 'Description': 'Shows tense hemorrhagic bullae on foot, oral mucosal lesions, histopathological findings, and immunofluorescence results'}}

An individual around 60 years old living in a rural area in the southeastern US was hospitalized with altered mental status, fever, flaccid paralysis of all extremities, and respiratory failure with lower lobe nodules and a left lower lobe infiltrate. Medical history included porphyria with polyneuropathy, alcohol use disorder, and a recent diagnosis of hyper-IgE syndrome, for which they received oral prednisone before admission.

Physical Examination: Patient presented with:- Altered mental status- Fever- Flaccid paralysis of all extremities- Respiratory distress

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': 'Bronchoalveolar lavage revealed:- Aspergillus fumigatus- Candida spp- Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pathological Findings': 'Autopsy findings:- Disseminated aspergillosis and candidiasis without ocular involvement- Multiple organ involvement including lungs, liver, pancreas, colon, appendix, and kidneys- Inflammatory infiltrates with lymphocytes, plasma cells, macrophages, and numerous eosinophils- Right eye: focal eosinophil-rich inflammatory infiltrate in posterior choroid with larval sections- Left eye: focal eosinophil-rich inflammatory infiltrate in posteronasal choroid', 'Imaging Studies': {'Image 1': {'Title': 'Posterotemporal Choroid of the Right Eye', 'Description': 'Focal eosinophil-rich inflammatory infiltrate with a cross-section of a possible degenerating larva'}, 'Image 2': {'Title': 'Posteronasal Choroid of the Right Eye', 'Description': 'Coiled, incompletely sectioned filariform larva within a medium-caliber artery'}}}

A man in his late 60s presented with a 4-week history of a rapidly growing left caruncular lesion. Medical and ocular histories were unremarkable.

Physical Examination: Ocular examination findings were normal except for an amelanotic, pedunculated mass with an ulcerated surface, arising from the caruncle. There was no evidence of PAM (primary acquired melanosis).

Diagnostic Tests: {'Histopathology': {'Gross Examination': 'The specimen measured 8 × 6 × 5 mm and had a 4-mm ulceration with necrotic tissue on its surface.', 'Microscopic Examination': 'Revealed a solid tumor with an inflamed crust on its surface, composed of large anaplastic cells with mitotic activity.', 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Showed positive reactivity to melanocytic markers HMB45, MITF2, and Melan-A. The Ki-67 proliferation marker showed a significant number of cycling cells.'}, 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Ulcerated, pedunculated, caruncular tumor', 'Figure B': 'Proteinaceous material on the ulcerated tumor surface (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×25)', 'Figure C': 'Mitotically active tumor containing anaplastic epithelioid cells (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×400)', 'Figure D': 'Melanoma cells showing immunoreactivity for Melan-A (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×100)', 'Figure E': 'Nuclear staining for Ki-67 reveals a high proliferative index (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×50)'}, 'Additional Tests': 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy findings were negative. Conjunctival and orbital biopsies revealed no tumor.'}

A girl younger than 2 years presented with a 1-year history of slightly pruritic lesions involving her face. She was born after an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery, and her growth and development were appropriate for her age. No associated symptoms such as fever, anorexia, or weight loss were noted.

Physical Examination: Multiple skin-colored and erythematous follicular papules, 1 to 2 mm in diameter, distributed in a segmental pattern on the right side of the forehead, as well as the dorsum and tip of the nose. A small, excoriated plaque was noted on the right malar area. No mucosal lesions were present, and the rest of the physical examination findings were unremarkable.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': 'Complete hematological and biochemical survey, including blood cell count, liver and renal function tests, C-reactive protein levels, thyroid function, rheumatoid factor, and antinuclear antibodies, disclosed no abnormalities.', 'Histopathological Examination': {'Findings': 'Accumulation of a finely granular basophilic material within the follicular and sebaceous gland epithelium. A moderate perivascular and periadnexal infiltrate of lymphocytes, lacking cytological atypia, was observed in the superficial and mid dermis. No epidermotropism nor vacuolar degeneration of the basal layer. Alcian blue stain demonstrated the presence of mucin.', 'Images': {'Figure 1': "Multiple skin-colored and erythematous follicular papules coalescing into a linear plaque involving the patient's forehead, as well as the dorsum and tip of the nose (A). Two skin punch biopsies from the forehead and nose (B and C)."}}}

A woman in her mid to late 70s with a history of immune thrombocytopenic purpura was admitted with:- Disturbed activity- Ataxic gait - Subacute onset of apathy- Severe disturbance of consciousness- Quadriplegia- Global dysphagia- NIH Stroke Scale score: 28 (indicating severe stroke)

Physical Examination: - Severe disturbance of consciousness- Quadriplegia - Global dysphagia- NIH Stroke Scale score: 28 (range 0-42)

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': ['- Platelet count: <5 × 103/µL before hospitalization', '- Cerebrospinal fluid analysis:         - Aβ40 level: 929 pg/mL (normal mean: 4003 ± 1185 pg/mL)         - Aβ40/Aβ42 ratio: 1.56 (normal mean: 4.91 ± 1.13)'], 'Imaging Studies': ['Brain CT Findings:       - Bleeding throughout the brain', '3-T MRI with Susceptibility Weighted Imaging (SWI):       - Abundant microbleeds and macrobleeds, particularly in lobar area       - Included lesions both visible and invisible on CT              Technical Parameters:       - Field strength: 3-T (MAGNETOM TrioTim; Siemens)       - Repetition time: 27 milliseconds       - Echo time: 20 milliseconds       - Slice thickness: 3 mm       - Gap width: 0 mm']}

A woman in her 60s presented with a slowly growing subcutaneous node in the right axilla that she first noticed 10 years ago, with progressive infiltration of the overlying skin over the past year. She reported experiencing swelling in the right axilla during pregnancy over 20 years ago that disappeared with lactation, followed by mild episodic pain in her right axilla during menstrual cycles.

Physical Examination: Solitary, indolent, erythematous plaque in the right axilla measuring 5 × 3 cm

Diagnostic Tests: {'Histopathology': {'Punch Biopsy': "Dermal tumor infiltrating the subcutaneous fat without epidermal involvement. Tumor cells diffusely dispersed between collagen bundles and lined up in an 'Indian file pattern' with basophilic nuclei and scant cytoplasm.", 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Positive for: pancytokeratin (AE1/AE3), cytokeratin 20, GCDFP15, estrogen receptor, and GATA3 antibodies. Negative for: synaptophysin, chromogranin, and S100-stainings.', 'Excision Findings': 'Multiple GATA3-positive and estrogen receptor–positive apocrine glands in close association to diffuse tumor cell infiltration', 'Lymph Node Analysis': '1 of the sentinel lymph nodes showed isolated cytokeratin8/18–positive tumor cells and MNF116-positive tumor cells'}, 'Imaging Studies': {'Staging': 'Chest, abdominal, and pelvic CT scans, cranial and breast MRI, mammography, and breast sonography revealed no evidence of primary or secondary tumors', 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Clinical photograph shows erythematous plaque in the right axilla measuring about 5 × 3 cm', 'Figure B': 'Histopathology from skin punch biopsy showing dense dermal infiltrates of atypical epithelioid tumor cells with basophilic nuclei dispersed between collagen fibers in an Indian file pattern', 'Figure C': 'Histopathology from complete tumor excision showing closely associated apocrine glands apart from diffuse infiltrates of tumor cells', 'Figure D': 'Immunohistochemistry demonstrating GATA3-positive apocrine glandular structures'}}}

A man in his 60s presented with an enlarging purpuric patch on his left lateral superior forehead and left anterior scalp, initially present for 2 weeks. Further discussion revealed the lesion first developed in March 2014 and was fairly stable until June 2014, when there was rapid growth and extension followed by central ulceration and bleeding.

Physical Examination: Clinical examination revealed a 30 × 25-cm centrally eroded purpuric plaque with multifocal peripheral reddish-purple patches, papules, and macules involving:- Left anterior scalp with extension posteriorly on left parietal scalp- Superior aspect of left occipital scalp- Anteriorly on left forehead and left lateral cheek- Medially to right frontal and right parietal scalpNo evidence of lymphadenopathy in preauricular, cervical, occipital, submandibular or supraclavicular areas.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Biopsy & Histopathology': '- Dermis permeated by irregular, slitlike spaces containing erythrocytes and lined by plump oval cells- Cells had pale eosinophilic cytoplasm and hyperchromatic nuclei- Cells stained positively with CD31 and were negative for CK5- Strong immunopositivity for ADRB1, ADRB2, and ADRB3 receptors- Ki-67 proliferation marker analysis performed', 'Imaging Studies': '- Chest and abdominal CT scan with contrast (June 2014): No evidence of nodal, pulmonary, or liver metastases- Staging: T2N0M0'}

A woman in her 30s with no relevant medical history was referred for a progressive bilateral nasal airway obstruction.

Physical Examination: Endoscopic examination found a voluminous, pulsatile, highly vascularized mass in both nasal cavities.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Imaging Studies': {'Endoscopic View': {'Title': 'Endoscopic View of the Right Nasal Cavity', 'Description': 'A hypervascular and pulsatile tumor occupies almost the entire right nasal fossa'}, 'CT Scan': {'Description': 'Enhanced CT scan showed a hypervascular tumor in the nasal cavity, on the left thyroid, the fifth rib, and the L4 vertebra'}, 'MRI': {'Description': 'Confirmed a hypervascular tumor with anterior skull base extension (contrast enhancement after gadolinium injection, intermediate T2 signal)'}, 'Radioiodine Scintigraphy': {'Description': 'Found considerable uptake regarding rib and vertebral lesions. The sinonasal tumor had low uptake, indicative of thyroid tissue with low iodine avidity'}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Serum Thyroglobulin': '4432 ng/mL (significantly elevated)', 'Biopsy Results': {'Costal Biopsy': 'Pointed to VTC metastasis', 'Sinonasal Tumor Biopsy': 'Confirmed VTC metastasis'}}}

A 7-year-old boy presented with 3 weeks of fever and rash that was refractory to treatment with antibiotics and systemic steroids. Prior to this, he had received a diagnosis of streptococcal pharyngitis and influenza A.

Physical Examination: The patient had fever and tachycardia. Skin examination revealed innumerable painful, necrotic papules that were coalescing into diffusely distributed ulcers.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': ['- Complete blood cell count: normal', '- Comprehensive metabolic panel: normal', '- C-reactive protein levels: elevated', '- Plasma cytokine profile (hospital day 4): elevated IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, interferon γ, and tumor necrosis factor α levels'], 'Pathology': ['Skin biopsy results on 3 occasions were consistent with PLEVA'], 'Images': {'Figure 1': {'Title': 'Clinical Presentation and Disease Progression', 'Description': 'A: Initial presentation. B: Progression of disease shortly after presentation. C: After 1 month of treatment. D: Five months after discharge.'}, 'Figure 2': {'Title': 'C-reactive Protein, Temperature, and Interferon γ Levels Throughout Hospitalization'}}}

A South Asian patient in their 60s with metastatic colon cancer developed multiple new dark nevi within 2 months of initiating encorafenib and panitumumab therapy. They reported the sudden appearance of new moles on the scalp, earlobe, face, palms, and soles.

Physical Examination: Examination of the volar surfaces revealed 3 atypical-appearing pigmented lesions. On dermoscopy, 2 of the smaller lesions revealed an atypical network and focal streaks. The largest lesion (12 × 9 mm) revealed a parallel ridge pattern (PRP).

Diagnostic Tests: {'Dermoscopy Images': {'Image Title': 'Dermoscopy Images of the 3 Eruptive Atypical-Appearing Volar Nevi', 'Image Description': 'Three lesions documented, with the largest showing parallel ridge pattern'}, 'Histopathological Findings': {'General Findings': 'Broad intraepidermal nested melanocytic proliferation with moderate cytologic atypia with occasional mitoses. Nests were located at the tips of the rete ridges, including the crista profunda intermedia, which contains the acrosyringia.', 'Special Stains': 'Fontana-Masson staining exhibited vertical columns of melanin predominantly overlying the CP intermedia', 'Immunohistochemistry': '- Negative for preferentially expressed antigen of melanoma and BRAF-V600E\n- p16 expression was lost\n- cyclin D1 results were positive'}}

A man in his 80s with a medical history of gastric adenocarcinoma treated with total gastrectomy presented with asymptomatic lesions on his tongue that gradually developed during the previous 3 months. He reported occasional alcohol intake, and there was no history of tobacco-related habits or oral trauma.

Physical Examination: Intraoral examination revealed multiple yellowish to erythematous papules and verrucous cobblestone-like plaques on the mucosal surface on both sides of his tongue. The skin of the face, neck, arms, and other sun-exposed areas did not reveal any associated lesions.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': 'Complete blood cell count, serum chemistry panel, liver and kidney function test, C-reactive protein levels, lipid profile, and serum and urine electrophoresis disclosed no abnormalities.', 'Histopathological Examination': {'Findings': 'Well circumscribed masses of a homogeneous eosinophilic material in the subepithelial connective tissue, underneath an atrophic epithelium. Prominent clefts separated the material, and scarce fibroblasts were also seen. The material showed no birefringence under polarized light microscopy, and a Congo red stain was negative for amyloid. Verhoeff–van Gieson and Orcein staining revealed fragmented elastic fibers at the periphery and within the colloid material.', 'Images': {'Figure A and B': 'Yellowish plaques in a cobblestone pattern on the right and left side of the tongue', 'Figure C and D': 'Circumscribed eosinophilic, amorphous fissured masses in the subepithelial connective tissue (hematoxylin-eosin)'}}}

A woman in her 50s presented with bilateral nasal obstruction, thick nasal discharge, and facial pain and pressure. Her condition was recalcitrant to 3 prior surgeries, immunotherapy, and medical therapy.

Physical Examination: Initial post-procedure examination showed:- Binocular horizontal diplopia with left lateral gaze- Inability for the left eye to track beyond the midline- Left eye was not proptotic, ecchymotic, or tense- Bedside intraocular pressure measurements: 15 OD, 16 OS (normal)- National Institutes of Health stroke score of 1

Diagnostic Tests: {'Clinical Images': {'Figure 1': {'Title': 'Clinical Images', 'Description': 'Images showing left-sided abducens nerve palsy immediately following surgery and its resolution by postoperative day 1'}, 'Figure 2': {'Title': 'Illustrative Representations of the Proposed Mechanisms', 'Description': 'Diagrams showing different hypotheses for abducens nerve palsy mechanisms following sphenopalatine ganglion blockade'}}, 'Emergency Evaluation': 'Workup results were negative for a cerebrovascular accident'}

A 15-year-old boy with biopsy-proven Crohn disease. He was initially on infliximab therapy (5 mg/kg subcutaneous injection every 8 weeks) and later switched to adalimumab (40-mg/0.8-mL subcutaneous injection every 2 weeks) due to disease relapse.

Physical Examination: - Bilateral scaly, thickened, and erythematous upper and lower eyelids- Both lower eyelids were ectropic- Visual acuity was 20/20 OU- Crusty plaques on the eyelid margins - Diffuse conjunctival hyperemia- Rest of the ophthalmic examination was unremarkable

Diagnostic Tests: {'Images': {'Title': 'Eyelid Manifestations Before and After Treatment', 'Description': 'A, Blepharitis and ectropion during TNF-α inhibitor treatment. B, Resolution after treatment discontinuation.'}}

A girl in her teens presented with a painful genital ulcer. She had noticed high-grade fever, nasal discharge, and cervical lymphadenopathy 3 weeks prior to the appearance of the genital lesion. Cefcapene pivoxil hydrochloride had been administered by her family physician, with no improvement. The patient had never had any sexual contact and had no history of genital herpes infection.

Physical Examination: On examination, she showed a severe aphtha, 25 mm in diameter, with marked edema on the left major labium. The ulcer had an overlying adherent gray-brown eschar.

Diagnostic Tests: {'Laboratory Tests': ['Leukocyte count: 5.3 × 109/L (normal, 3.5-9.0 × 109/L)', 'C-reactive protein concentration: 1.2 mg/dL (normal, <0.3 mg/dL)', 'Alanine aminotransferase level: 276 IU/L (normal, <3-30 IU/L)', 'Serologic test results for hepatitis B and C viruses, HIV, and Treponema pallidum: all negative', 'Anti-IgM antibody titer against M fermentans: 1.12 EIU (normal, 0.09-0.47 EIU)', 'Bacterial cultures of the lesion: no significant isolates'], 'Imaging Studies': [{'Test Type': 'Chest radiography', 'Result': 'No abnormalities'}, {'Image Title': 'Clinical Image of Acute Genital Ulcer in Case 1', 'Image Description': 'A necrotic ulcer overlying adherent gray-brown eschar on the edematous major labium'}]}

A woman in her 30s with a history of atopy. Past medical history includes inadequate response to phototherapy, methotrexate, adalimumab, and ustekinumab treatments. The patient developed flexural eczema on the wrists and antecubital and popliteal fossa concurrent with treatment. The condition resolved during pregnancy while on cyclosporine for 18 months, but recurred within 8 weeks after resuming treatment postpartum.

Physical Examination: Flexural eczema present on:- Wrists- Antecubital fossa- Popliteal fossa

Diagnostic Tests: {'Clinical Scoring': 'Baseline PASI score: 19.4', 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Image showing chronic plaque psoriasis on the left arm of patient 2', 'Figure B': 'Image showing development of eczema on the left arm of the same patient following therapy'}}

A woman in her 40s with a medical history of multiple sclerosis presented with sudden development of 2 adjacent nodules on the left arm. The patient had undergone an excision with 1-cm margins of the left arm and a left axillary sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB) 3 weeks prior under general anesthesia. The patient was being treated with subcutaneous interferon beta 1a for multiple sclerosis.

